Article 9: The scope of compensation for ship-source oil pollution damage includes:
(1) expenses incurred for taking preventive measures to prevent or mitigate ship-source oil pollution damage, and further loss or damage caused by such preventive measures;
(2) damage to property other than the ship caused by the ship-source oil pollution accident, and the resultant loss of income;
(3) loss of income caused by environmental damage due to the oil pollution;
(4) expenses for reasonable restoration measures that have been taken or are to be taken with respect to the polluted environment.

Article 14: Where marine fishery, coastal tourism, and other sea-use or coastal business units or individuals request compensation for loss of income suffered due to environmental pollution, and all of the following conditions are met, thereby proving that there is a direct causal relationship between the loss of income and the environmental pollution, the People's Court shall support the request:
(1) the claimant's production and business activities are located in or near the polluted area;
(2) the claimant's production and business activities mainly rely on the polluted resources or coastline;
(3) it is difficult for the claimant to find other substitute resources or business opportunities;
(4) the claimant's production and business operations belong to a relatively stable local industry.
